Programming - writing your own programs in JavaScript
=====================================================

You can write programs for this desktop in JavaScript, and run them by
double-clicking them, as you would BASIC programs. Two books show you
how. They're in the Manuals directory; read them with !Bookworm by
double-clicking their Start files.


Programming in JavaScript
-------------------------

  Manuals.JSTutor.Start       (examples in Examples.JS)

For people who have done a little programming before, in BASIC say.
It starts with a program that says hello and works up to games with
windows, the mouse and the keyboard, finishing with !Snake. Along the
way it teaches the JavaScript language and the desktop's programming
interface: windows, icons, menus, drawing and files.


Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript
------------------------------------------

  Manuals.JSApps.Start        (examples in Examples.JSApps)

The follow-on book. You build two complete RISC OS applications:

  - !Contacts, an address book with a toolbar, a list you can sort and
    search, cards to edit, vCard and CSV files, undo and Choices;
  - !Organiser, a personal organiser in the style of Lotus Organizer:
    a ring binder with Diary, To do, Address, Notepad, Planner and
    Anniversary sections, alarms, printing and iCalendar files.

Each chapter's stage is a complete application in Examples.JSApps, so
you can start from any of them.


A complete game to read
-----------------------

  Diversions.!Lander2         (see the guide Lander2)

Lander II, a Zarch-like game grown from David Braben's Lander, is
written in JavaScript in the same way, as about thirty files in its
application directory: a 3D renderer, a flight model, aliens, sound
effects made from nothing, menus and high scores. Each file starts
with notes saying what it does. Choose View source on its title page
to read it in !JsEdit, and try changing it (on a copy).

  Apps.!Journal               (see the guide Journal)

The Journal, a diary with a page a day, is a desktop application
written the same way, with the toolkit from the second book (Form,
ListView, Dates): a calendar drawn in a window, pages you type into
(the desktop's TextArea), Find, a Save box for each export, a Draw
file made from nothing, and pages locked with a password. Its Check
program tests the parts that don't need windows.


What you need
-------------

  - !JsEdit (in Apps), the editor the books use: see the guide JsEdit.
    (!Edit works too.)
  - Programs are files of type JSScript: double-click one to run it.
    Errors are reported with the line they happened on.
  - The command *JSRun <file> runs one from the command line (F12).

The interface programs use is the one the desktop's own applications
are written with.
